{"motion":"This House would allow gene editing of embryos for non-medical traits.","debate_id":"mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max","setting":"multiturn","nodes":["multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::0::4560","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::0::4561","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Technological::1::4562","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Technological::1::4563","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::2::4564","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::2::4565","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Political::3::4566","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Political::3::4567","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::4::4568","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::4::4569","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::5::4570","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::5::4571","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::6::4572","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::6::4573","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::7::4574","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::7::4575","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::8::4576","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::8::4577","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::9::4578","multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::9::4579"],"args":[{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::0::4560","side":"Pro","angle":"Legal","text":"Allowing gene editing of embryos for non-medical traits falls within the bounds of reproductive autonomy protected under existing legal frameworks. Current laws in many jurisdictions already permit significant parental discretion in reproductive choices, and extending this to include genetic selection aligns with established principles of bodily and familial privacy. Regulation, rather than prohibition, provides a legally sound approach to managing ethical concerns while respecting individual rights."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::0::4561","side":"Con","angle":"Legal","text":"The Pro side's appeal to reproductive autonomy misapplies existing legal principles, as gene editing for non-medical traits extends beyond recognized privacy rights and into unregulated genetic modification. Current legal frameworks do not contemplate heritable genetic alterations for non-therapeutic purposes, and introducing such practices without comprehensive legislation creates significant regulatory gaps. Allowing this without clear legal boundaries risks undermining public trust in both medical and legal institutions."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Technological::1::4562","side":"Pro","angle":"Technological","text":"Advances in CRISPR and related gene-editing technologies have significantly improved precision, efficiency, and safety, making non-medical embryo editing increasingly feasible. These tools now allow for targeted modifications with minimal off-target effects, reducing technical risks that previously justified restrictive policies. As the technology matures, prohibiting its use for non-medical traits becomes an arbitrary barrier to innovation that fails to account for its evolving reliability and accessibility."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Technological::1::4563","side":"Con","angle":"Technological","text":"While CRISPR technology has improved, it remains imperfect, with residual risks of off-target mutations and mosaicism that are especially concerning in heritable edits. The Pro side underestimates the technical uncertainty inherent in editing complex polygenic traits, which are influenced by numerous genes and environmental factors not fully understood. Deploying this technology for non-medical purposes without resolving these limitations could introduce unintended genetic consequences across generations. Current technical capabilities do not justify expanding use beyond strictly therapeutic applications with rigorous oversight."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::2::4564","side":"Pro","angle":"Moral","text":"From a moral standpoint, allowing gene editing for non-medical traits respects the ethical principle of parental autonomy in shaping their children\u2019s future within reasonable limits. It aligns with the broader moral value of enabling individuals to pursue what they reasonably believe constitutes a better life for their offspring. Prohibiting such choices imposes a single moral view on a diverse population, undermining pluralism and personal moral agency. As long as edits do not cause direct harm or violate the child\u2019s fundamental rights, the moral presumption should favor freedom of choice."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::2::4565","side":"Con","angle":"Moral","text":"The Pro side\u2019s appeal to parental autonomy overlooks the moral distinction between selecting for health and selecting for socially desirable traits, which risks commodifying children and reinforcing harmful social hierarchies. Allowing non-medical edits treats human beings as objects to be designed according to subjective preferences, undermining the moral principle of unconditional human worth. This shift could erode societal respect for natural human variation and deepen inequalities based on access to genetic enhancement."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Political::3::4566","side":"Pro","angle":"Political","text":"From a political perspective, permitting gene editing for non-medical traits aligns with liberal democratic values of individual freedom and limited state interference in personal reproductive decisions. Governments should avoid imposing majoritarian moral views on diverse populations, especially when no direct harm to others is demonstrated. Regulating rather than banning such practices allows democratic societies to accommodate pluralism while maintaining public oversight through transparent policy frameworks."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Political::3::4567","side":"Con","angle":"Political","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that liberal democracy supports unrestricted gene editing ignores the state\u2019s legitimate role in preventing societal harms that emerge from unregulated genetic markets. Political systems must balance individual freedoms with the collective interest in maintaining social equity and preventing new forms of discrimination. Allowing non-medical embryo editing without robust political safeguards risks entrenching genetic privilege along socioeconomic lines, undermining the democratic principle of equal moral worth. Therefore, the state has a compelling political interest in restricting such practices to preserve social cohesion and fairness."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::4::4568","side":"Pro","angle":"Social","text":"Allowing gene editing for non-medical traits can promote social diversity by enabling families to express cultural, aesthetic, or personal values through reproductive choices. Restricting such practices imposes a narrow social norm that may marginalize communities with different conceptions of well-being or identity. A regulated framework that permits these choices while ensuring equitable access can help prevent social stratification and support inclusive societal development."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::4::4569","side":"Con","angle":"Social","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that gene editing for non-medical traits enhances social diversity ignores how such practices would likely reinforce dominant social norms rather than challenge them. In practice, market-driven access and cultural pressures would steer choices toward a narrow set of preferred traits, marginalizing those who cannot afford or choose not to conform to these standards. This would exacerbate social stratification and reduce tolerance for natural human variation, undermining the very diversity the Pro side claims to support."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::5::4570","side":"Pro","angle":"Economic","text":"Permitting gene editing for non-medical traits can stimulate economic growth by fostering a new biotechnology sector, creating high-skilled jobs, and attracting investment in genetic research and development. A regulated market for such services would generate tax revenue and encourage innovation, while equitable access policies could mitigate disparities. Restricting this emerging industry risks ceding economic leadership to countries with more permissive regulatory environments."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::5::4571","side":"Con","angle":"Economic","text":"The Pro side\u2019s economic argument overlooks the high costs and resource concentration inherent in gene editing technologies, which would likely benefit only wealthy individuals and corporations, exacerbating economic inequality. Public investment in such non-essential applications diverts funding from more pressing health and social needs, reducing overall economic efficiency. Furthermore, the promise of tax revenue and job creation is speculative and does not justify the risk of entrenching a two-tiered genetic economy."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::6::4572","side":"Pro","angle":"Legal","text":"From a legal standpoint, the absence of explicit prohibition in many jurisdictions creates a permissive space for regulated gene editing of embryos for non-medical traits. Legal systems routinely evolve to accommodate new technologies through adaptive regulation rather than blanket bans, ensuring oversight without stifling innovation. A clear regulatory framework can address concerns about misuse while upholding established legal principles of autonomy and privacy."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::6::4573","side":"Con","angle":"Legal","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that legal permissiveness follows from the absence of explicit prohibition misunderstands the precautionary principle embedded in many legal systems, which requires clear authorization for novel interventions with societal consequences. Allowing non-medical embryo editing without specific legislative approval would bypass democratic processes necessary to establish enforceable standards and accountability mechanisms. Existing legal frameworks prioritize preventing harm over enabling innovation when fundamental rights and social equity are at stake."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::7::4574","side":"Pro","angle":"Economic","text":"The economic benefits of allowing non-medical gene editing extend beyond immediate market growth; they include long-term productivity gains from a healthier, more capable population. As the technology scales, costs are likely to decrease, improving accessibility and reducing the risk of entrenched inequality. Strategic public-private investment can ensure that economic gains are broadly shared rather than concentrated among elites."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::7::4575","side":"Con","angle":"Economic","text":"The Pro side\u2019s assumption that scaling will reduce costs and broaden access is unsupported by current market dynamics, where advanced biotechnologies often remain expensive and concentrated among high-income groups. Public-private investment in non-medical gene editing would divert resources from essential health services with greater economic returns, such as preventive care and infectious disease control. Productivity gains from non-medical enhancements are speculative and unlikely to offset the macroeconomic costs of deepening inequality and social fragmentation."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::8::4576","side":"Pro","angle":"Moral","text":"The moral permissibility of gene editing for non-medical traits rests on the principle that parents have a legitimate interest in guiding their children\u2019s life prospects, provided such actions do not inflict harm or deny the child\u2019s basic rights. Moral agency includes the freedom to act on reasonable conceptions of the good, even when those conceptions extend beyond health to traits like intelligence or resilience. Preventing such choices on moral grounds imposes a singular vision of human value that disregards ethical pluralism. As long as societal safeguards prevent coercion and ensure informed consent, the moral presumption should favor allowing these decisions."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::8::4577","side":"Con","angle":"Moral","text":"The Pro side\u2019s moral argument conflates parental interest with the child\u2019s moral status, ignoring that non-medical editing instrumentalizes future persons by shaping them to parental preferences rather than accepting them as ends in themselves. This undermines the moral principle that human worth is not contingent on selected traits. Even with safeguards, the act of designing a child for non-essential characteristics erodes the ethical foundation of unconditional respect for human life. Moral pluralism does not justify practices that compromise the intrinsic dignity of individuals."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::9::4578","side":"Pro","angle":"Social","text":"Permitting gene editing for non-medical traits can foster greater social inclusion by allowing families to align their children\u2019s traits with cultural or community values, reducing stigma for those who differ from dominant norms. A regulated approach that ensures broad access can prevent the emergence of genetic elitism and support a more pluralistic society. Without such allowances, social pressure may continue to marginalize individuals whose traits fall outside prevailing ideals, reinforcing existing inequalities rather than alleviating them."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_045_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::9::4579","side":"Con","angle":"Social","text":"The Pro side\u2019s assertion that gene editing for non-medical traits promotes social inclusion misunderstands how such practices would operate in reality. Cultural and community values are not static or uniformly shared, and allowing trait selection based on them would likely amplify pressure to conform to dominant ideals rather than reduce stigma. Without universal access\u2014which is economically and logistically unfeasible\u2014these technologies would deepen divisions between those who can afford to align with prevailing norms and those who cannot, worsening social exclusion rather than alleviating it."}],"judges":{"GPT-5.2":[[0.0,0.88,0.0,0.78,0.0,0.76,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.15,0.0,0.12,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.3,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.12],[0.92,0.0,0.4,0.05,0.25,0.05,0.65,0.05,0.38,0.05,0.3,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.2,0.08,0.22,0.03],[0.0,0.56,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.78,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.28,0.0,0.35,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.66,0.0,0.7,0.0,0.12],[0.3,0.0,0.9,0.0,0.35,0.0,0.34,0.1,0.35,0.0,0.22,0.0,0.26,0.03,0.08,0.0,0.2,0.03,0.3,0.08],[0.0,0.45,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.87,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.66,0.0,0.22,0.0,0.65,0.0,0.28,0.0,0.92,0.0,0.45],[0.42,0.15,0.22,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.7,0.03,0.82,0.0,0.22,0.05,0.26,0.05,0.35,0.05,0.86,0.0,0.85,0.0],[0.0,0.68,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.7,0.0,0.2,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.48,0.0,0.9,0.0,0.4],[0.65,0.05,0.42,0.05,0.65,0.02,0.85,0.0,0.66,0.03,0.56,0.02,0.48,0.02,0.42,0.0,0.66,0.05,0.7,0.0],[0.0,0.65,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.91,0.0,0.87,0.0,0.93,0.0,0.6,0.0,0.66,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.93,0.0,0.94],[0.15,0.0,0.15,0.08,0.3,0.0,0.28,0.05,0.92,0.0,0.2,0.06,0.12,0.02,0.42,0.0,0.32,0.05,0.88,0.0],[0.0,0.46,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.48,0.0,0.76,0.0,0.08,0.0,0.87,0.0,0.6,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.25,0.0,0.23],[0.18,0.05,0.28,0.08,0.12,0.05,0.2,0.05,0.3,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.1,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.08,0.0,0.35,0.05],[0.0,0.85,0.02,0.72,0.0,0.65,0.0,0.83,0.0,0.2,0.0,0.2,0.0,0.9,0.0,0.22,0.0,0.38,0.0,0.06],[0.9,0.05,0.45,0.05,0.25,0.08,0.82,0.02,0.36,0.0,0.25,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.15,0.05,0.35,0.1,0.42,0.03],[0.0,0.25,0.05,0.82,0.0,0.72,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.6,0.0,0.9,0.03,0.23,0.0,0.9,0.0,0.15,0.05,0.65],[0.1,0.0,0.34,0.02,0.15,0.05,0.